<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bethlehem</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bethlehem is a Palestinian city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>, about 10 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> it is the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s family, and Christian tradition holds it to be the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, marked by the Church of the Nativity, first built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> obtained it by treaty from the Ayyubid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">al-Kamil</a> in 1229.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bethlehem.jpg" alt="Bethlehem" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Neil Ward · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_the_Nativity_(7703592746).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bethlehem stands in the Judean Mountains at about 775 metres above sea level, some 30 metres higher than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, which lies about ten kilometres to the north. The Arabic name Bēt laḥm means house of meat, while the Hebrew Bet Leḥem means house of bread or house of food. Beit Jala and Beit Sahour form an urban cluster with the city, and the refugee camps of Aida and Azza lie within its limits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A possible earliest reference is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> correspondence of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdi-heba">Abdi-Heba</a>, the Egyptian-appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, appeals for help in retaking a town near Jerusalem called Bit-Lahmi after attacks by Apiru mercenaries. The reading is uncertain and has met with objections. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> names Bethlehem as the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>. In 2012 excavators in the City of David found a clay seal impression reading From the town of Bethlehem to the King, which they date to the 8th or 7th century BCE and take to have sealed a tax shipment.</p><p>The Gospel of Matthew and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a> place the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in Bethlehem, though scholars regard the two accounts as contradictory and are divided on his actual birthplace. Under the Roman Empire Hadrian destroyed the city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> later commissioned the Church of the Nativity in 327 CE. It was heavily damaged in 529 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> in revolt and rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>. In 1009 the Fatimid caliph al-Hakim bi-Amr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ordered it demolished, but local Muslims, who were permitted to worship in its southern transept, spared it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> took Bethlehem in 1099 and replaced the Greek Orthodox clergy with Catholic ones. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> demolished its walls in the mid-13th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> rebuilt them in the 16th. From 1517 the Catholic and Greek Orthodox churches bitterly disputed custody of the basilica. Bethlehem was part of Mandatory Palestine from 1920 to 1948, was held by Jordan after the 1948 Arab–Israeli War and was captured by Israel in 1967. Refugees from the 1947–48 war settled near the city and helped turn its Christian majority into a Muslim one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bethlehem is the capital of the Bethlehem Governorate and had 28,591 inhabitants in 2017. Its economy is closely tied to tourism and to pilgrimage to the Church of the Nativity at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>, and it is known for olive wood and mother-of-pearl carving, a craft said to have been introduced by Franciscan friars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the 15th century. Bethlehem University, founded in 1973, was the first university established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bethlehem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…called Asomtavruli, was in use by the fifth century, as shown by inscriptions at Bir el Qutt near <b>Bethlehem</b> and at the Bolnisi Sioni church, dated 493–494, and the Martyrdom of the Holy Queen Shushanik, written in the late fifth century, is the oldest surviving work of Georgian…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When Godfrey died in July 1100, his brother Baldwin, count of Edessa, came south and was crowned King Baldwin I at <b>Bethlehem</b> on Christmas Day 1100.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Judah’s territory covered the southern highlands around Jerusalem, Hebron and <b>Bethlehem</b>, the low hills of the Shephelah to the west and the arid Negev to the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its octagonal plan may have been influenced by the Byzantine-era Church of the Seat of Mary, known as Kathisma in Greek, built between 451 and 458 on the road between Jerusalem and <b>Bethlehem</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nativity is told in Matthew and Luke, which agree that Jesus was born in <b>Bethlehem</b> in Judea in the reign of King Herod; reckoning from the death of Herod would place the birth between 6 and 4 BCE, but neither account names a day.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nativity is told by Matthew and Luke, who agree that he was born in <b>Bethlehem</b> in the reign of King Herod, to Mary, and that the conception was divinely effected.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
